    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 326.583, 485.46, 349.384 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We say that a quiver Q is a connected sum of two full subquivers Q′ and Q′′ along a vertex i ∈ Q0 if"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 294.989, 404.158, 321.784 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(Q′)0 ∪ (Q′′)0 = Q0 and (Q′)0 ∩ (Q′′)0 = {i}. In this case, we write"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 268.949, 335.638, 293.704 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Q = Q′#iQ′′. Given a dimension vector d for Q, we write"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 235.319, 252.497, 376.678, 263.464 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(Q,d) = (Q′,d|Q′)#i(Q′′,d|Q′′)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 224.789, 485.261, 246.632 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We call such a connected sum of quiver settings thin if di = 1. In the following, the symbol ↔ denotes an arrow of either orientation."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 171.149, 485.33, 216.992 ],
    "font" : "CMTI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Theorem 4.2. For a connected quiver setting (Q,d), the representation space Rd(Q) is a spherical variety under the action of Bd ⊂ Gd if and only if (Q,d) is a thin connect sum of quiver settings with Q of the form 1 ↔ 2 ↔ 3, and d = (m,n,1) or d = (m,2,n)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.719, 141.509, 485.285, 163.472 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. First assume Rd(Q) is spherical. We reduce to the claimed situation in several steps, always using the previous lemma."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 24,
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 143.639, 91.589, 485.476, 137.432 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(1) If Q contains a cycle, then Rd(Q) even admits inﬁnitely many Gd-orbits, since there are already inﬁnitely many isomorphism classes of representations of dimension vector (1,...,1) for any cycle quiver. Thus we can assume Q to be a tree."
  } ]
